# Helpdesk/Ticketing System: Project Rules

## What this project is
A full-stack helpdesk/ticketing system built as a portfolio project. The owner is learning, so clarity matters more than cleverness.

## Stack (strict, do not substitute or add alternatives)
- Backend: Node.js + Express with TypeScript, in /server
- Database: PostgreSQL, accessed through Prisma (schema, migrations, queries)
- Frontend: React + TypeScript built with Vite, in /client
- Styling: Tailwind CSS only (no other UI or component libraries)
- Auth: JWT access tokens + refresh tokens, passwords hashed with argon2 or bcrypt
- Validation: Zod on every endpoint
- Docs: OpenAPI/Swagger
- Tests: Jest + Supertest (backend, using a separate test database); Vitest + React Testing Library (frontend)
- Infrastructure: Docker Compose (api, web, postgres)
- Lint/format: ESLint + Prettier in both /server and /client

Do not install a new package without telling me why it is needed first.

## Code explanation rules (apply to every file)
- Put a comment on every line of code, explaining in plain, simple language what it does.
- Start every file with a header comment that explains, for a beginner:
  1. What the file is for
  2. How it fits into the rest of the project
  3. Any new concept it uses (e.g. middleware, JWT, migration, foreign key), with a simple everyday analogy
- Avoid jargon. Define any technical term the first time it appears.
- Keep explanations accurate. Simple wording is fine, but never say something untrue to simplify.
- For files that cannot hold comments (e.g. package.json), explain them in the chat reply.

## Roles and permissions
- Requester: create tickets; view, comment on, and close only their own tickets
- Agent: view tickets assigned to their team; update status; assign to self; add public or internal comments
- Admin: everything above, plus manage users, roles, teams, categories, priorities, and SLA rules
- All permissions are enforced on the backend (Express middleware). The frontend may hide or disable actions, but the backend is the source of truth.
- Internal comments must never be returned to requesters, in any endpoint.
- Deactivated users cannot log in or use existing tokens.

## Ticket workflow
- Statuses: Open -> In Progress -> Waiting on Requester -> Resolved -> Closed, plus Reopened
- Reject invalid status transitions on the server.
- Every status change and its audit log entry are saved in one database transaction.
- The allowed transitions live in one place (docs/workflow.md and a single constants file), not scattered across the code.

## Conventions
- Every endpoint validates input with Zod and returns errors in one consistent format: { "error": { "code": "...", "message": "..." } }
- Never put secrets in code. Use .env, keep it in .gitignore, and commit only .env.example.
- Rate-limit auth endpoints, use Helmet, and configure CORS for the Vite dev origin.
- Use async/await, descriptive names, and small functions. Keep route handlers thin and put logic in service files.
- Keep controllers, services, routes, and middleware in separate folders.

## How to work
- Work on one phase per task. Do not start the next phase on your own.
- Before writing code for a phase, read the files in /docs.
- Before changing the database schema or permission rules, list the files and tables that will be affected and wait for my approval.
- Write tests for permission rules and status transitions before implementing them.
- After implementing, run the tests and show me the real output. Never say something works without running it.
- Keep /docs up to date: data-model.md, permissions.md, workflow.md, api.md.
- At the end of each phase, tell me: what was built, how to run it, how to test it, and a short "what I learned in this phase" summary in plain language.
- If a request is unclear or conflicts with these rules, ask before guessing.